#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bedc359-7d2a-5f82-9f14-82db352d3d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374679} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed42872-9fbb-5c01-8ee4-255df2140180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374680} {:answer/id #uuid "1c14b9ee-e58f-516c-a1a3-195c750de2e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374681} {:answer/id #uuid "c62f2b00-4f47-52db-969d-136479e33863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ed42872-9fbb-5c01-8ee4-255df2140180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374680} :answer-field/id #uuid "169b79ce-78f5-5ef8-9444-bf12ad43c40b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374678} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d8dedfa-471d-59a7-8091-efb46ea4b269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374684} {:answer/id #uuid "f7445fe8-1f60-5e7c-8946-f2fba75ed1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374685} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5c0342-42c3-5238-ae21-bbd3dfde382a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374686} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d70310-c65a-5730-ae59-75a2269cd75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f5c0342-42c3-5238-ae21-bbd3dfde382a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374686} :answer-field/id #uuid "4887b11f-1d5c-5578-9029-c147fe171be0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74bbe1ee-844e-5190-9dbf-b11d0159e1fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-338921" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25\\lambda y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n=0,1,2,\\ldots.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R\\setminus\\{0\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+25\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 5\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+5B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-5A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n5\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$\n\nIndexing and nonzero-amplitude clarification: In the positive-eigenvalue case above, $n=1,2,3,\\ldots$. The separate zero case contributes $\\lambda_0=0$ with $y_0(x)=B_0$, where $B_0\\ne0$. Thus the complete spectrum and eigenfunctions are $\\lambda_n=n^2/25$ and $y_n(x)=B_n\\cos(nx)$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ and $B_n\\ne0$. Since $5\\sqrt{\\lambda_n}=n$ for these indices, this is exactly the accepted displayed formula $B_n\\cos(5x\\sqrt{\\lambda_n})$. These explicit indices avoid assuming a convention for the source symbol $\\mathbb N$; a zero amplitude would produce the trivial solution, which is not an eigenfunction."}]}
